ny  of them are clinicians who  need to see patients and yet they're  incredibly dedicated toward  um making this work happen on their  weekends and their evenings and in  in on the side and so while it's a  challenge because a lot of these  uh activities are not in the natural  flow of the hospitals or the  institutions  while there are a lot of challenges with  respect to uh  logistics and governance um you know the  people  and the ability of the people to be  dedicated and to work really hard  and to uh make it happen really  surmounts  all of these these limitations and so i  guess i think in terms of the positives  the people who actually make it happen  uh and not in the negatives which all  can be  um as in our experience overcome  thank you now how is this data inform  clinical trials going forward  um i think that you know clinical trials  in africa are  uh challenging because a lot of the  infrastructure not only the regulatory  infrastructure but the clinical  infrastructure to do example for example  a phase one trial where very intensive  um monitoring and laboratory uh  activities may be required uh but also  access to drugs  um so there's a lot of challenges of  doing this in africa but i think that  the  main thing that i would say the research  contributes to clinical trials is our  understanding  that patients are incredibly diverse uh  the cancers that we're studying in  africans  um look different than the cancers we're  studying in  american white populations european  populations and so  by understanding these underlying  ideological differences as well as the  characteristics the molecular  characteristics of tumors  uh in diverse african populations really  helps us to understand that  developing a clinical trial in a  homogeneous  white population is valuable it's  important obviously but it's  missing a lot of the important knowledge  we may gain from doing a clinical trial  when we exclude more diverse populations  and i don't even just mean african  populations but  uh latinx african-american  asian populations uh that are not as  much part of clinical trials as the  majority populations at least in the  united states  so increasing the diversity our  background knowledge of the diversity  informing um our the need  to address that diversity in uh  clinical research is something that i  think we are  the this kind of research really makes  us aware of and  motivates the need for diverse inclusion  and accrual of clinical trial  participants  dr rebic thank you so much for this q a  session we have time for one  more question but i do want to let our  audience know that we have so many  questions come in that we are unable to  answer  but we want to let you know that these  questions will be answered via the  contact information you provided  at the time of registration so to wrap  dr rebic how long will these cohorts be  followed up in their life  are there defendants to be tested  uh well we hope uh with adequate funding  and  longevity of the investigators to follow  these  uh patients up and follow and pursue the  research in a lot of different  directions  so we have not yet really undertaken  family studies although we're starting  to do that now  we certainly hope to follow up the  patients to uh monitor  uh treatment outcomes and uh mortality  and  and vital status and things like that so  we certainly have  uh short to intermediate uh follow-up uh  interests but we really hope to expand  these studies in a lot of other  directions including  more biologically oriented studies  mechanistically  based studies and interventional studies  particularly in the area of screening  and early detection  one of the problems in africa of course  is very late presentation because of  lack of screening so we're certainly  thinking about  how to implement screening strategies  which in prostate cancer have been very  difficult and very fraught  in the u.s for example but is a great  need in africa so i think that the  direction that we're taking these is  really toward  using the basic uh epidemiological and  causation knowledge we're getting to  prevent early detect  cancers and reduce mortality as a result  thank you and dr rabbit can you i want  to squeeze one more question in because  this question has come in a few times  is there an application process for the  new centers that would like to join this  initiative  uh well email me there's a we have a  website  uh www.madcapnetwork.org  on that website is a lot of information  about sim our  studies we also have a link below with  an email address that people can contact  us we're very happy to have uh  collaborators join both as  contributors to the science but also as  our data become available  they will be made public they will be  shareable through a process that we have  the genomic data are all being deposited  uh  on a uh you know in a public space so  that people will have access to the data  so there are a lot of opportunities both  to collaborate and in the future to use  our data uh for  other uh studies uh in a lot of  different directions so we  absolutely welcome uh collaboration and  have people  visit our website and learn more and  contact us  dr rebbik thank you so much for your  time today and your important research  we would also like to thank robert and  our sponsor thermo fisher scientific for  underwriting today's educational webcast  now before we go i do want to thank our  audience again for joining us and for  their interesting questions  we had a numerous amount of questions  that we weren't able to answer and again  those will be answered via the email  address you provided  at the time of registration today's  webcast  can be viewed on demand and lab routes  will alert you via email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  that's all for now we thank you for  joining us  and we hope to see you again soon have a  great day
 

